## A question no argument can settle

In October 1950 the British philosophy journal Mind published a paper by the mathematician Alan Turing, “Computing Machinery and Intelligence.” Its first sentence is still quoted today: “I propose to consider the question, ‘Can machines think?’”

Almost at once Turing walks away from it. To answer, you’d first have to agree on what “machine” and “think” mean. If you go by how people use the words, he points out, the answer would have to come from an opinion poll, and that’s absurd. You can argue about words forever and never check anything.

So Turing changes the question. Instead of asking whether the machine thinks, he asks whether it can behave in conversation so that nobody can tell it from a person. That move is the heart of the paper, and of this lesson.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: why didn’t Turing answer the original question?</summary>

Its words are vague, and the answer would come down to a poll of opinions. He replaced it with a question about behavior, whose outcome can be checked.

</details>

## The imitation game

Turing describes a parlor game for three: a man (A), a woman (B) and a judge (C) in another room. The judge questions them both and has to work out which is the woman. The man tries to mislead the judge; the woman tries to help. So that no voice or handwriting gives the game away, the answers come back typed, ideally over a teleprinter.

Now a machine takes the man’s place. Turing’s question becomes: will the judge be wrong as often as when two people were playing? If so, we have a testable stand-in for the question about thinking.

Later this game came to be called the Turing test. Notice two things about it. The judge compares two players; it isn’t a chat with one. And the verdict rests on text alone: nobody looks inside the machine.

Switch the diagram between set-ups and see how your chat with a chatbot differs from Turing’s game.

<ImitationGame />

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: what does the judge see in the imitation game?</summary>

Only the typed answers of two hidden players. The judge can’t see or hear them, and compares one with the other.

</details>

## A modest prediction

Turing didn’t claim the machines of his day could win. He made a forecast, and it’s more careful than most retellings. In about fifty years, he wrote, computers with a memory of about a billion (10 to the 9th power) binary digits would play so well that an average judge, after five minutes of questions, would pick right no more than 70 times in 100.

In the same breath he called the original question too meaningless to deserve discussion, and predicted that by the end of the century people would speak of machines thinking without expecting to be contradicted. That second prediction is about language, not machines: about how people’s habits would change.

Notice what the forecast leaves out. No “machines will be smarter than us.” No “machines will become conscious.” Just five minutes, an average judge, and 70 percent.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: which three numbers are in Turing’s prediction?</summary>

About fifty years, five minutes of questions, and no more than 70% right guesses by the judge.

</details>

## Objections he answered in advance

More than half the paper answers critics. Turing works through nine objections, from the theological to telepathy. Three of them are still with us.

**A machine only does what it’s told.** This is Ada Lovelace’s point, made about Babbage’s Analytical Engine. Turing replies that machines surprise him all the time: a programmer can’t foresee every consequence of a program.

**What matters isn’t behavior but whether the machine feels anything.** Turing takes this argument from consciousness from a 1949 lecture by the neurosurgeon Geoffrey Jefferson. His answer: we judge whether other people think only by how they behave. Strictly, only the thinker knows for sure. In everyday life we keep a polite convention that everyone thinks.

**An adult mind is too complex to program.** Here Turing agrees, and suggests a way round: build a “child machine” with little built in, and educate it. Seventy years later, that is roughly how language models are made: not programmed with rules, but trained on text.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: how did Turing answer the argument from consciousness?</summary>

We judge other people’s thinking only by their behavior too. Demand more of a machine, and you’d have to demand the same of people.

</details>

## The test today: what “passed” means

In 2014 the University of Reading announced in London that a chatbot called “Eugene Goostman” had passed the Turing test: 33% of 30 judges took it for a person. The bot played a 13-year-old boy, whose mistakes and odd answers were easy to forgive. Critics pointed out that the 30% in Turing’s forecast had been turned into a pass mark the paper never sets, and that the teenage persona simply lowered the judges’ expectations.

In 2025 Cameron Jones and Benjamin Bergen of the University of California, San Diego ran a strict version of the game: a judge chats with a person and a model at the same time and decides which is which. GPT-4.5, told in detail what kind of person to play, was judged to be the human in 73% of conversations, more often than the real people. For comparison, GPT-4o scored 21%, and ELIZA, a program from 1966, 23%.

What follows from that? Exactly what the game measures: in a five-minute text chat, a machine can no longer be told from a person. That’s an important fact, and it’s about behavior. Turing never promised that such a machine understands, feels or tells the truth.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: why was the “Eugene Goostman” result criticized?</summary>

The 30% from a forecast was passed off as the test’s rule, and the teenage persona excused mistakes in advance. Five minutes with a “child” is a weak check.

</details>

## What this means for you

Every time you talk to a chatbot, you’re in the judge’s seat, in an easier game: one partner, and nobody to compare it with. Turing left us an honest rule: judge by behavior, not by guesses about what’s inside. The rule has a second half, though: judge by the behavior you actually care about.

If you need the truth, don’t grade how fluent and human an answer sounds. That’s precisely where models are now strongest. Grade what can be checked: facts, references, sums, whether the code runs. Sounding like a person and being right are two different questions, and the imitation game answers only the first.

## Do it now

1. Open any chatbot and ask it three questions you know the answers to. Note where it sounded sure, and where it was right.
2. Find a headline saying something “passed the Turing test,” and ask: how long was the conversation, who judged, and what was the machine compared with?
3. Press “Finish the lesson” so the cards go into Review.
